{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_xm7qfelasyat6jctczlgfg34g7gbkvwvptltfkt3toxqxk4anelq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification complete. All four phases pass.\n\n## Result\n\n**The Rust translation is byte-identical to the C for all 2^32 possible inputs** — proven exhaustively, not sampled.\n\nThe library is tiny (one function, `float2half`, plus two 512-entry lookup tables), so I was able to make coverage complete rather than probabilistic.\n\n### Completion gate\n\n| Gate | Status |\n|---|---|\n| `SYMBOLS.md` — `nm -D` symbol diff | ✅ empty; 0 non-libc undefined in Rust |\n| Phase B — every `CONFIGS.md` row (C1–C39) | ✅ 10 tests |\n| Phase C — every `ERRORS.md` row (E1–E17) | ✅ 7 tests |\n| Binary stdout comparison | n/a — neither project builds an executable (`CMakeLists.txt` has no `add_executable`; no `[[bin]]`) |\n| All feature combinations | ✅ crate declares no `[features]`; swept by script and asserted by a test |\n\n**Exhaustiveness argument:** the result is exactly `base[j] + (mantissa >> shift[j])`, so it depends on the input *only* through the pair `(j, mantissa >> shift[j])`. Enumerating all 512 buckets × every attainable quotient, with both all-zero and all-one discarded low bits, is behaviourally exhaustive over the whole 2^32 input space. Plus 2.5M randomized inputs on top.\n\n### Two things worth flagging\n\n**1. The C truncates; it is not a round-to-nearest conversion.** I wrote a test expecting `float2half(65520.0) == 0x7c00` (infinity), which is what a normal float→half conversion gives. The C returns `0x7bff`. **I corrected the test, not the C** — the entire binade `[65504, 65536)` maps to `0x7bff`, and the jump to infinity happens at exactly `65536.0`. This is recorded in `CONFIGS.md`, and `mutation_check.sh` carries a \"round-to-nearest fix\" mutant so nobody can later \"correct\" this behaviour unnoticed.\n\n**2. I mutation-tested the suite, because 21 passing tests in 0.04s is not by itself evidence of anything.** 7/7 observable mutants were caught. Two survivors are *provably* equivalent, not gaps: `u16::saturating_add` never triggers because the max attainable sum is exactly `0xffff` (asserted for all 512 buckets), and `shift 23 → 24` is unobservable because a 23-bit mantissa `>> 23` and `>> 24` are both always zero.\n\n### One incident to report\n\nWhile mutation testing, my backup path used `$TMPDIR`, which **changes between Bash calls** in this environment. The backup silently failed, so five mutations accumulated into `src/lib.rs` and the restore failed too — leaving the file corrupted, with no git repo to recover from. I rebuilt it by **generating both tables directly from `c_src/src/lib.c`** and verified all 1024 entries match the C element-by-element. The file is now correct and arguably more trustworthy than before, but it was recovered rather than restored, so the code comments are my reconstruction. `mutation_check.sh` now backs up inside the working dir and verifies the restore by checksum.\n\nTwo smaller notes: the crates.io registry is unreachable here, so cargo commands need `--offline` (libloading 0.8.9 was already in the local cache). And `c_src/` is untouched — timestamps still show the originals."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7440,"cache_read_input_tokens":103193,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":7440,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c7f3c01d-01fe-4e20-b4cb-2997c0686154","uuid":"ca7fb599-91f5-4284-a2e2-99d336a8ac45","timestamp":"2026-09-05T00:37:38.708Z"}
